        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 362 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And God saw the light, that it was good: and God divided the light from the darkness&#x27; Ephrem takes as the establishment of the elemental moral framework that the entire Scripture presupposes. The dividing (the Syriac fres = to separate definitively) is not provisional but ordinative: light and darkness are henceforth bounded by divine decree. Ephrem reads the &#x27;goodness&#x27; (the Syriac tabbutha) as not merely aesthetic approval but the recognition that the light corresponds to the divine intent — God names what He has done as fitting. This pattern (creation + recognition + naming) Ephrem extracts as the divine workflow that the believer is called to imitate: every legitimate work is done, recognized, and named for what it is.</p></aside>',
