    (
        3,
        19,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (362).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 362 AD)</small><p>&#x27;In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, till thou return unto the ground&#x27; Ephrem reads as the imposition of mortality + the dignification of labor. Mortality enters as judgment (&#x27;thou shalt return&#x27;); but labor is given as both consequence-of-fall AND mercy-toward-fallen-humanity — work occupies the soul that would otherwise turn to deeper rebellion in idleness. The &#x27;sweat of thy face&#x27; Ephrem reads as marking labor with the dignity of effort: post-fall human work is genuinely costly and genuinely productive. The Tewahedo monastic tradition&#x27;s emphasis on manual labor (the Egyptian-Syriac desert father heritage) inherits this Ephremic reading: work is ascetic gift, not curse to be evaded.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis III.19 (NPNF S2 V13). PD.",
    ),
