    (
        11,
        4,
        "f",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (362).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 362 AD)</small><p>The Babel-builders&#x27; resolution — &#x27;let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven; and let us make us a name&#x27; — Ephrem reads as the prototype of every subsequent self-deifying human project. The &#x27;tower whose top may reach unto heaven&#x27; Ephrem takes as the inversion of the rightful divine order: instead of God descending to dwell with humanity (as He will at Sinai, in the Temple, and ultimately in the Incarnation), humanity attempts to ascend to heaven by its own engineering. The &#x27;let us make us a name&#x27; Ephrem identifies as the deepest motive: the desire for autonomous self-significance apart from divine naming. The Tewahedo theological reading of Babel maintains this Ephremic anti-Promethean frame: every human construct that aims at deity by self-effort is doomed to confusion.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis XI.4 (NPNF S2 V13). PD.",
    ),
    (
        11,
        4,
        "g",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>TOWERS</strong> of Babel (Genesis 11:4), Edar (Genesis 35:21), Penuel (Judges 8:9, 17), Shechem (9:46), David (Cant. 4:4), Lebanon (7:4), Syene (Ezekiel 29:10), Hananeel (Zechariah 14:10), Siloam (Luke 13:4). There were several towers in Jerusalem (2 Chronicles 26:9; Psalm 48:12). They were erected for various purposes, as watch-towers in vineyard (Isaiah 5:2; Matthew 21:33) and towers for defence.",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
