        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Neither shall thy name any more be called Abram, but thy name shall be Abraham; for a father of many nations have I made thee&#x27;. Ephrem reads the divine name-change as a SACRAMENTAL renaming: the addition of the heh (the divine breath / spirit-letter, also added to Sarai → Sarah) signifies the entry of the divine name itself into Abraham&#x27;s identity. Tewahedo baptismal liturgy preserves the name-given-at-baptism tradition (the newly-baptised receives a Christian saint-name) as the sacramental continuation of this Abrahamic name-change pattern.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XVII.5. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
