        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>The Akedah opens with &#x27;And it came to pass after these things, that God did tempt Abraham&#x27;. Ephrem reads the temptation as PROBATIVE not deceptive — God already knows Abraham&#x27;s heart; the test is FOR ABRAHAM, so that Abraham may know what was already in him. Tewahedo spiritual-formation theology (preserved in monastic ascetical texts) follows Ephrem: every trial reveals the soul TO ITSELF; God permits the testing so that the tested know their own depths.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXII.1. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
