    (
        32,
        24,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And Jacob was left alone; and there wrestled a man with him until the breaking of the day&#x27;. Ephrem reads the mysterious wrestler as the pre-incarnate Word — the same Person who later appears to Joshua as captain of the Lord&#x27;s host (Josh 5:13-15) and who ultimately becomes incarnate in Mary&#x27;s womb. Tewahedo Christology preserves the wrestling-with-the-pre-incarnate-Word as the canonical OT Christophany that establishes the principle: theophany in the OT is always the Son, never the Father.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXXII.24. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
    ),
    (
        32,
        24,
        "e",
        "",
        "dict-easton",
        "Dictionary",
        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>PENUEL</strong> face of God, a place not far from Succoth, on the east of the Jordan and north of the river Jabbok. It is also called “Peniel.” Here Jacob wrestled (Genesis 32:24-32) “with a man” (“the angel”, Hos. 12:4. Jacob says of him, “I have seen God face to face”) “till the break of day.” A town was afterwards built there (Judges 8:8; 1 Kings 12:25). The men of this place refused to succour Gideon and his little army when they were in pursuit of the Midianites (Judges 8:1-21). On his return, Gideon slew the men of this city and razed its lofty watch-tower to the ground.",
        "Easton's Illustrated Bible Dictionary, M. G. Easton (1897). Public domain.",
    ),
