        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And he turned himself about from them, and wept; and returned to them again&#x27;. Ephrem treats Joseph&#x27;s hidden weeping as the model of concealed-compassion: Joseph maintains the testing-disguise BUT the compassion is real and ongoing. Tewahedo pastoral-theology preserves this image: shepherds and confessors may need to maintain apparent severity for the penitent&#x27;s good, but the compassion underneath must always be active.</p></aside>',
