        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Now therefore be not grieved, nor angry with yourselves, that ye sold me hither: for God did send me before you to preserve life&#x27;. Ephrem reads this verse as the FOUNDATIONAL providence text — God&#x27;s salvific intent runs through and ABOVE human evil intent. Tewahedo providence-theology (especially developed in monastic spiritual direction for those processing past harms) draws on Gen 45:5: the harm was real; God&#x27;s purpose ran deeper than the harm.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XLV.5. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
