        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;But as for you, ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good, to bring to pass, as it is this day, to save much people alive&#x27;. Ephrem treats this verse as the providence-formula par excellence — the complete two-tier reading of history. Tewahedo providential-theology cites Gen 50:20 in close conjunction with Rom 8:28 (&#x27;all things work together for good to them that love God&#x27;) as the OT-NT providence-pair. The verse is foundational in Tewahedo consolation literature: every personal harm submits to the larger divine purpose without thereby being justified or made non-harmful.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section L.20. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
